{"name":"Operetta Mauve","hex":"#3a284c","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"operetta","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A lighter version of opera with a frivolous story and spoken dialogue. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/operetta"},{"term":"mauve","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A rich purple synthetic dye, which faded easily, briefly popular c. 1859‒1873 and now called mauveine.","A pale purple or violet colour, like the colour of the dye after it has faded."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having a pale purple colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/mauve"}],"entries":[{"title":"Operetta","blocks":[{"text":"Operetta is a form of theatre and a genre of light opera. It includes spoken dialogue, songs, and dances. It is lighter than opera in terms of its music, orchestral size, and length of the work. Apart from its shorter length, the operetta is usually of a light and amusing character. The subject matter may portray \"lovers' spats, mistaken identities, sudden reversals of fortune, and glittering parties\". It sometimes also includes satirical commentaries."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/71/Bouffes_parisiens_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Bouffes_parisiens_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operetta","wikidata":"Q170384"},{"title":"Mauve","blocks":[{"text":"Mauve is a pale purple color named after the mallow flower. The first use of the word mauve as a color was in 1796–1798 according to the Oxford English Dictionary, but its use seems to have been rare before 1859. Another name for the color is mallow, with the first recorded use of mallow as a color name in English in 1611."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/32/Wilde_Malve.JPG/330px-Wilde_Malve.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mauve","wikidata":"Q604079"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/RitaB.jpg/330px-RitaB.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/RitaB.jpg/330px-RitaB.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A Columbia Records advertisement for a recording of Rita Montaner in a production of Eliseo Grenet and Ernesto Lecuona's Niña Rita, o, La Habana en 1830, an operetta from the Spanish genre of zarzuela","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARitaB.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/Boccaccio_cover_page.jpg/500px-Boccaccio_cover_page.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/Boccaccio_cover_page.jpg/1280px-Boccaccio_cover_page.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/Boccaccio_cover_page.jpg/500px-Boccaccio_cover_page.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Cover page of Boccaccio, oder Der Prinz von Palermo (Boccaccio, or the Prince of Palermo) by Franz von Suppé in 1879. An example of early Viennese operetta.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABoccaccio_cover_page.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/1878_poster_for_Jacques_Offenbach%27s_Orph%C3%A9e_aux_enfers.jpg/500px-1878_poster_for_Jacques_Offenbach%27s_Orph%C3%A9e_aux_enfers.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/1878_poster_for_Jacques_Offenbach%27s_Orph%C3%A9e_aux_enfers.jpg/1280px-1878_poster_for_Jacques_Offenbach%27s_Orph%C3%A9e_aux_enfers.jpg?utm_